      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Saxitoxin, a guanidinium-rich neurotoxin, was detected in the brain and medulla oblongata of cats, showing that guanidinium-containing molecules can cross the BBB.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "AMD3100, a dicationic bicyclam, crossed the BBB with a small but measurable amount in CSF, indicating that dicationic compounds can have some CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is guanethidine, a dicationic guanidine-azocane with very low lipophilicity and full ionization at physiological pH. Although some guanidine-containing and dicationic analogues show measurable brain uptake, guanethidine is a known peripheral sympatholytic that does not cross the blood-brain barrier to a meaningful extent. The physicochemical prior (low passive permeability, high efflux) and established pharmacology outweigh the limited transferable evidence from structurally distinct analogues.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct experimental brain/plasma or CSF data for the query molecule itself.",
    "The transferable evidence comes from structurally different guanidines (zwitterionic cyclocreatine, polycyclic saxitoxin) or much larger dicationic macrocycles (AMD3100), limiting direct applicability.",
    "No information on specific transporter involvement (e.g., OCT, NET) or efflux liability for the query at the BBB."
  ],
